+      <h2>Two config layers — and your vault carries its own</h2>
+      <p class="sub">ob-sync keeps device stuff on the device and vault stuff inside the vault. That split is what makes multi-device sync boring — in the best possible way.</p>
+      <div class="grid g2">
+        <div class="plat">
+          <h3>🖥️ Device config — <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">~/.config/ob-sync/config</code></h3>
+          <p>Describes <b>this device</b>: which vault is pinned, where backups live, branch overrides. Written by <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">ob-sync init</code>, editable via menu <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">17) EditConf</code> — and it never leaves the machine. Environment variables (<code>OBS_VAULT</code>, <code>OBS_REMOTE</code>, …) always outrank it.</p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="plat">
+          <h3>🪪 Vault identity card — <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">&lt;vault&gt;/.ob-sync/config</code> <span class="tag">new in 9.5</span></h3>
+          <p>Describes <b>the vault</b>: its <code>REMOTE</code> and <code>BRANCH</code> — two keys, nothing else. Written by <code>init</code> / <code>remote</code>, committed with the vault, and re-aligned with <code>origin</code> on every locked run: the card wins over a stale origin.</p>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:18px">
+        <div class="codeblk"><button class="copy" type="button">copy</button><pre># .ob-sync/config — committed with the vault
+REMOTE=git@github.com:you/MyVault.git
+BRANCH=main</pre></div>
+        <div class="codeblk"><button class="copy" type="button">copy</button><pre>git clone git@github.com:you/MyVault.git
+ob-sync init      # adopts the clone — remote already known
+ob-sync sync      # green on the first run</pre></div>
+      </div>
+      <p class="sub" style="margin-top:18px">When several sources disagree, the order is fixed: <code>OBS_REMOTE</code> → the vault's <code>origin</code> → the identity card → the device config (only for its own pinned vault). A vault with none of these refuses honestly and <code>init</code> asks — nothing is ever guessed. Even a deleted <code>.git</code> is recoverable: <code>ob-sync repair</code> rebuilds the metadata from the card's remote.</p>
+      <div class="note"><b>🔐 No tokens, ever.</b> Neither file stores credentials — only URLs and branch names. Log in once with git's credential helper (<code style="font-family:var(--mono)">git config --global credential.helper store</code> or <code style="font-family:var(--mono)">gh auth login</code>) and every device, cron job and widget reuses it. An automated test asserts the card stays credential-free.</div>
